LikeOS-64
=========

A modern 64-bit operating system built from scratch, featuring a Unix-like
userland with process management, syscalls, multitasking, SMP support, a
multi-user login, bash as the shell, an ext4 root filesystem, TCP/IP
networking with OpenSSH, and an X.Org desktop.

Current state
-------------

- Real hardware support: tested on Lenovo and Dell notebooks with USB boot.
- QEMU support: run with USB image targets, XHCI enabled and VMSVGA graphics.
- VMware support: USB 3.1 boot.
- VirtualBox support: xHCI (USB 3) and VMSVGA graphics.
- UEFI boot is required.
- Linux bootstrapper support: boot from a Linux USB host and launch LikeOS in
  embedded QEMU. Note: not strictly required -- the supported targets are VMs
  and real hardware, both of which run LikeOS independently of Linux.

Overview
--------

LikeOS-64 is a Unix-like operating system demonstrating modern OS development:

- Complete userland: ring 3 processes with syscall interface.
- Process management: preemptive multitasking scheduler.
- SMP support: BSP and AP startup with per-CPU scheduling data.
- UEFI bootloader: GNU-EFI based ELF64 kernel loading.
- Filesystems: ext4 (the root filesystem) and FAT32, with VFS and devfs.
- Caching strategy: dentry cache, inode cache, and page cache.
- Demand paging: pages are mapped when they are first touched, not at exec.
- Multi-user: login with PAM and yescrypt, users, groups, and DAC permission
  enforcement in the VFS.
- Networking: TCP/IP stack with BSD-style sockets and multiple NIC drivers.
- Graphics: X.Org server on the framebuffer, with a window manager, a terminal,
  a text editor and a web browser.
- Unicode: UTF-8 end to end -- console, C library, terminal emulator and X.
- Hardware drivers: xHCI USB 3.0, USB mass storage, PS/2, I2C-HID (unfinished),
  PCI, APIC, ACPI, and VMware SVGA II.
- Memory: physical/virtual memory, SLAB allocator, stack canaries, and
  security protections.
- Virtual terminals, TTY, PTYs and signals: VTs with POSIX-like terminal
  and signal handling.
- Threads: POSIX threads with mutexes, condition variables and TLS.
- Diagnostics: kernel and user crashlog printing with BUG_ON/WARN_ON asserts.

Feature summary
---------------

Core OS
~~~~~~~
- 64-bit long mode kernel and higher-half mapping.
- UEFI boot path with framebuffer and memory map handoff.
- Interrupt and exception handling with IDT and PIC/APIC integration.

SMP (Symmetric Multi-Processing)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- Up to 64 CPUs.
- AP startup using INIT-SIPI-SIPI and AP trampoline.
- Per-CPU data structures via GS base.
- Per-CPU run queues and scheduler accounting.
- LAPIC timer setup and SMP IPI support.
- IOAPIC routing for multi-core delivery.
- TLB shootdown helpers and SMP-aware locking paths.

Networking
~~~~~~~~~~
- TCP/IP stack: Ethernet, ARP, IPv4, ICMP, IGMP, UDP, and TCP.
- BSD-style socket layer, plus Unix domain sockets.
- DHCP client, DNS resolver, and routing table.
- Softirq-based packet processing with rate limiting.
- NIC drivers: Intel e1000/e1000e/igb, eepro100, AMD PCnet32, NE2000,
  Realtek RTL8139, DEC Tulip, and VMware vmxnet3.
- Networking utilities: ifconfig, route, arp, ping, traceroute, netstat,
  dhclient, host, dig, nslookup, hostname, and arping.

Filesystems
~~~~~~~~~~~
- ext4 is the root filesystem: extents, hash-indexed (htree) directories,
  extended attributes, and a journal with ordered-data commits.  Both read and
  write paths are implemented, including extent-tree growth and directory
  hashing, and the bootloader reads /boot/kernel.elf straight out of it.
- FAT32 for the EFI System Partition and removable media (mounting removable media is still a pending feature).
- VFS layer with symlinks, hard links, file locking, and chroot.
- devfs: /dev/null, /dev/zero, /dev/random, /dev/urandom, /dev/tty, /dev/pts,
  /dev/fb0, /dev/input/event*, and the disk nodes.

Memory management
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- Physical page allocator with a bitmap and per-page reference counts.
- Four-level paging, higher-half kernel, and a RAM-sized direct map.
- SLAB allocator for kernel objects.
- Demand paging: anonymous memory, brk, BSS and private file-backed mmap are
  populated on first touch rather than at exec time, so a large program starts
  without faulting in what it never reads.
- Copy-on-write fork, mmap/munmap/mprotect, and guard-paged stacks.
- System V and POSIX shared memory (used by X clients through MIT-SHM).

Users, login and permissions
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- init -> getty -> login, driven by /etc/inittab.
- PAM stack with a yescrypt-based crypt(3); /etc/passwd, /etc/shadow,
  /etc/group.
- Real, effective and saved user and group IDs, supplementary groups, and
  set-user-ID execution.
- Discretionary access control enforced in the VFS, with a single privilege
  chokepoint, so file permissions apply to every path that opens a file.
- Account tools: id, whoami, groups, su, passwd, adduser, addgroup, deluser,
  delgroup.

Graphics and input
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- Framebuffer console with scrollback, a scrollbar, and a mouse pointer.
- VMware SVGA II driver with mode setting and a hardware cursor; the UEFI GOP
  framebuffer is the fallback on hardware without it.
- /dev/fb0 (fbdev) and /dev/input/event* (evdev), which is what the X server
  drives.
- PS/2 keyboard and mouse, USB HID, and I2C-HID touchpads.

Unicode
~~~~~~~
- UTF-8 is the encoding everywhere: the console decodes it and addresses glyphs
  by code point, the terminal emulator tracks character widths, and the C
  library implements the full multibyte and wide-character interface.
- Console font is PSF2-capable PSF1 with a Unicode table (Uni2-Terminus16,
  512 glyphs covering 791 code points: Latin, Greek, Cyrillic, box drawing).
- Character classification, case mapping and display widths come from tables
  generated against a reference implementation, and are checked against it over
  every code point (host/test-unicode.sh).

Caching strategy
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- Dentry cache:
  - Name lookup cache for directory entries.
  - Positive and negative dentry entries.
  - Hash buckets with LRU eviction.
- Inode cache:
  - Metadata cache keyed by inode number.
  - Reference counting and dirty metadata writeback.
  - Cached cluster chain acceleration for FAT32.
- Page cache:
  - File data pages (4 KiB) with hash-indexed lookup.
  - Read-ahead and sequential access optimization.
  - CLOCK-style eviction and dirty writeback handling.

Process management and userland
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- fork, execve, exit, wait4, signals, pipes.
- User mode execution in ring 3.
- ELF64 user binary loading, plus #! script execution.
- Userland libc and dynamic runtime linker (ld-likeos.so) with dlopen/dlsym.
- POSIX threads: mutexes, condition variables, barriers, thread-local storage,
  and futex-based blocking.
- poll, select and epoll; PTYs for terminal multiplexers and remote logins.
- POSIX regular expressions, iconv, and locale support.

Virtual terminals and TTY
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- POSIX-like TTY line discipline and job-control signals.
- ANSI/VT100/VT220/xterm escape sequence emulation, including the alternate
  screen, scroll regions and mouse reporting, so full-screen programs work.
- Pseudo-terminals, which is what tmux, xterm and sshd sessions run on.
- Per-task TTY output routing for clean crashlog and diagnostics printing.

Security and hardening
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- Stack canaries (stack-smashing protection) in kernel and userland.
- User/kernel address-space separation and ring 3 isolation.
- Page-level memory protections.

Reliability and diagnostics
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- Proper crashlog printing for both kernel and user faults.
- In-kernel assertion framework: BUG_ON, WARN_ON, WARN_ON_ONCE,
  VM_BUG_ON/VM_WARN_ON, and BUILD_BUG_ON.
- Non-fatal warnings continue execution; fatal bugs print a full crash dump.

User core utilities
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Included commands (selection):

- Shell: bash 5.2 is /bin/sh -- job control, history, completion, globbing,
  arrays and arithmetic, all of it.
- Core navigation: ls, pwd, cd behavior in shell.
- File tools: cat, cp, mv, rm, mkdir, rmdir, touch, stat, file, find, ln,
  chmod, readlink.
- Text tools: grep, sed, expr, wc, sort, uniq, cut, tr, head, tail, more, less,
  strings, hexdump, nano.
- System tools: uname, uptime, date, time, ps, top, df, du, free, dmesg.
- Process tools: kill, sleep.
- Environment tools: env, which, echo, printf.
- Power tools: shutdown, poweroff, reboot, halt.
- Network tools: ifconfig, route, arp, ping, traceroute, netstat, dhclient,
  host, dig, nslookup, hostname, arping.
- Account tools: id, whoami, groups, su, passwd, adduser, addgroup, deluser,
  delgroup.
- Other utilities: clear, man, tty, yes, true, false.
- Test programs: test_syscalls, test_libc, testmem, teststress, progerr.

Ported applications
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Third-party software ported to LikeOS-64 (under ports/).  Sources are kept
byte-identical to upstream; everything that differs is a build file or a patch
under the port's own directory.

Shell and terminal:

- bash 5.2: the system shell, installed as /bin/sh.
- tmux: terminal multiplexer.
- nano: text editor.
- ncurses: terminal handling (a LikeOS implementation of the API).

Network and crypto:

- OpenSSH 10.4: ssh, scp, sftp, ssh-keygen, ssh-agent and the rest of the
  client tools, plus sshd -- so the machine can be logged into over the
  network as well as out from.
- OpenSSL 3.5: TLS and crypto library and toolkit.
- curl 8.14: HTTP/HTTPS and friends.
- netcat (OpenBSD): networking utility.
- libevent, zlib, nghttp2: supporting libraries.

X Window System (ports/xorg, ~70 packages):

- X.Org server 21.1 on the framebuffer, with the evdev input driver and the
  fbdev video driver.  Installed setuid root and drops privileges as soon as
  its devices are open, so no user needs the video or input groups.
- ctwm: window manager, with a desktop bar and root menu.
- xterm: terminal emulator (UTF-8, 256 colour).
- NetSurf: web browser, with its own layout and rendering engine.
- XNEdit: Motif text editor.
- xcalc, xclock, xload, xsetroot, xrandr, xset, xauth: the usual X clients.
- Toolkits and libraries: libX11, libXt, libXaw, libXft, Motif, freetype,
  fontconfig, pixman, libpng, libjpeg, xkbcomp and xkeyboard-config.

Build
-----

Prerequisites (Ubuntu/Debian):

  sudo apt update
  sudo apt install gcc nasm xorriso mtools dosfstools ovmf gnu-efi-dev \
                   debootstrap parted gdisk qemu-utils grub-efi-amd64-bin rsync

Or:

  make deps

Build all:

  make all

Main build outputs:

- build/bootloader.efi
- build/kernel.elf
- build/bin/* user programs
- build/likeos-ext4.img   single bootable GPT USB disk:
                          P1 = FAT EFI System Partition (bootloader only),
                          P2 = ext4 root (/boot/kernel.elf + the whole userland).
                          The bootloader loads the kernel straight from ext4;
                          there is no FAT data filesystem anywhere else.

Run
---

QEMU:

  make distclean
  make qemu          # plain boot from the ext4 GPT disk
  make qemu-usb      # same, plus SLIRP networking (sudo)

Logging in
~~~~~~~~~~
At the login prompt, log in as

  login: root
  password: toor

Change it with passwd, and create an ordinary account with adduser -- the
system is multi-user and file permissions are enforced, so you not always want to be root.

Starting the graphical desktop
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
From the shell, run

  startx

That brings up the X.Org server with the ctwm window manager, a clock and a
load graph in the desktop bar.  Button 1 on the desktop background opens the
root menu: xterm, xcalc, XNEdit, NetSurf, and "Exit session" to come back to
the console.

Any user can run startx -- the server is installed setuid root and gives root
up as soon as it has opened the framebuffer and the input devices, so no group
membership is needed.

Screen resolution
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
The preferred resolution is chosen at build time:

  make                         1920x1200 preferred (the default)
                               (falls back to 1920x1080, 1280x800, 1280x768,
                               1152x864, 1024x768)

  make SCREEN_SIZE=medium      1280x800 preferred
                               (falls back to 1280x768, 1024x768)

Both ends of that list are a preference rather than a demand, which is why the
larger one is the default: the fallback chain reaches down to 1024x768, so
asking for a mode the display cannot do costs nothing, while asking for the
smaller one on a display that can do better caps it there.

Both the bootloader and the kernel's SVGA driver use the same table, so the
setting applies whether the mode comes from UEFI or from a mode set later.

Other useful targets:

  make distclean
  make usb-write USB_DEVICE=/dev/sdX
  make qemu-realusb USB_DEVICE=/dev/sdb

  make SCREEN_SIZE=medium      lower preferred resolution (see above)
  make help                    every target and build variable

Booting a real USB stick in a virtual machine
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  make qemu-realusb USB_DEVICE=/dev/sdb

boots QEMU straight from a stick written by usb-write, which is the way to
check that the written stick really boots.

This target is console only -- do not run startx under it.  Every disk read
goes to physical USB hardware through an emulated controller, and X, which
reads tens of megabytes of libraries, fonts and keymaps before it draws
anything, is far too slow to be usable that way.

QEMU is the awkward way to do this.  VMware and VirtualBox both boot a real
stick with X working and need none of what follows -- point the VM at the USB
device and go.  See "Current state" at the top of this file for what each of
them supports.

Under QEMU, running X against a real stick is a little tricky: it needs KVM,
and it is worth driving QEMU through virt-manager rather than by hand.  This
configuration is known to work:

  1.  Create a new virtual machine in virt-manager.
  2.  Select manual installation.
  3.  Choose Debian 13 as the operating system.
  4.  Give it 2048 MB of RAM and, for example, 4 CPUs.
  5.  Tick "Customize configuration before install".
  6.  Under Overview, set Firmware to UEFI with OVMF_CODE_4M.fd.
  7.  For the NIC, select Bridge and enter your bridge device (virbr0 here).
  8.  Select e1000e as the NIC model first, then edit the model to e1000 in
      the XML.
  9.  Set Video to VGA.
  10. Boot the machine, connect the external USB device also in qemu, and type "exit" at
      the UEFI boot prompt.

LikeOS then boots from the stick.  Log in as root with the password toor and
run startx.

Linux USB host mode (not really needed, for historic reasons):

  make distclean
  make linux-usb
  make linux-usb-write USB_DEVICE=/dev/sdX

Project structure
-----------------

- boot/: UEFI bootloader and trampoline.
- kernel/: kernel subsystems (ke, hal, mm, fs, io, dev, net).
- include/kernel/: public kernel headers.
- user/bin/: user programs (core utilities and tests).
- user/lib/libc/: C library and runtime pieces.
- user/lib/rtld/: dynamic linker.
- res/: files staged into the image (fonts, /etc, X configuration).
- host/: build-host tools (Linux USB builder, table generators, tests).
- ports/: third-party ports, including ports/xorg for the X Window System.

Implementation status
---------------------

Completed:
- UEFI bootloader and ELF64 loading.
- Multitasking scheduler and userland process model.
- SMP bring-up and per-CPU kernel infrastructure.
- Syscall layer, signals, pipes, virtual terminals, TTY, and PTYs.
- ext4 (read/write, journalled) as the root filesystem; FAT32; VFS; devfs;
  and the caching layers.
- Demand paging and copy-on-write fork.
- Multi-user login: init/getty/login, PAM, yescrypt, and VFS-enforced DAC.
- xHCI USB stack and USB mass storage.
- TCP/IP networking stack, sockets, and multiple NIC drivers.
- POSIX threads, shared memory, poll/select/epoll.
- UTF-8 and Unicode throughout the console, the C library and X.
- X.Org server on the framebuffer, with ctwm, xterm, NetSurf and XNEdit.
- Stack canaries and crashlog/assertion (BUG_ON/WARN_ON) infrastructure.
- Framebuffer console optimizations and a VMware SVGA II driver.
- Userland libc, dynamic linker, and the core utilities set.
- Ports: bash, OpenSSH, OpenSSL, curl, tmux, nano, netcat, and X.Org.

Planned/future work:
- More drivers (AHCI/NVMe, more GPUs).
- Expanded POSIX coverage.
- Package management.
- GTK3 port

